{
  "id": "v39-site-spellings",
  "asserts": "One site, one spelling, two matchers. Every form `sensorium ts run --focus` accepts is a form `watch --at` accepts -- the bare qualname, its owner, the file's stem, its basename and its root-relative path -- so a reader who focused a function can ask about it in the words they already typed. Every form the focus resolver refuses is refused here too: a prefix that does not end at a boundary (`Fogs`, `Fog.compute.x`), a member name without its owner (`compute`), a directory (`lib`) and a path this trace does not hold (`src/cache.ts`). The ten rows are `typescript/test/fixtures/site-spellings.json`, which the JS matcher's test reads as well: the rule has two implementations by design and one example table, so neither can drift. A spec that matches nothing is answered with the trace's own sites, each spelled the same way -- never the dotted module name, which is Python's and which no TypeScript reader would type.",
